      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Certificate number: R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01\n- Scheme: This is an OIML certificate of conformity. The scheme A|B — OIML-CS Basic (A) vs MAA (B). This is a regular OIML certificate from 2011, before MAA. Actually the schema says scheme (A|B). Hmm. Typical OIML-CS: Scheme A (Basic system) and B (MAA). Older certificates like this... The certificate doesn't explicitly state the scheme. I'll use \"A\" as default for basic OIML certificate system.\n- Project number: 10200858\n- Page total: 7\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 2 May 2011 → 2011-05-02\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (The Issuing Authority NL1)\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, The Netherlands (also PO Box 394, 3300 AJ Dordrecht, NL)\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Title: Head Certification Board\n- Phone: +31 78 6332332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\n**Applicant:**\n- KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\n**Manufacturer:**\n- KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: Water meter (not in examples but appropriate — \"Water meter\")\n- Type designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- Description: Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, model \"WATERFLUX 3070\", class 1 and 2. Based on electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- R49 — actually this certificate covers R49-1/2006, R49-2/2006, R49-3/2006. Canonical ID: \"R49\". Edition: 2006? The schema says edition (int). R49-1/2006 → edition 2006. Amendment: null. Scheme: A. Accuracy classes: [1, 2].\n\n**Test reports:**\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01, 41 pages + 14 annexes, role: test_report\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01, 40 pages + 3 annexes, role: test_report\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01, 40 pages + 4 annexes, role: test_report\n\nWait, the pages field — \"includes 41 pages and 14 annexes\". Pages: 41 (annexes separate). I could note pages: 41. Hmm, I could put \"41 + 14 annexes\" but schema says pages. I'll use integer 41 and maybe mention annexes elsewhere? The schema only has id, date, pages, role. I'll put pages: 41 etc. Actually maybe safest: pages: 41. Dates not given for test reports, so omit.\n\n**Now the model family — this is the crux.**\n\nThe tables span pages 3-7. Page 3: DN65 (class 1), DN80 (×2 variants), DN100 (×2 variants), DN125 (×2 variants) — class 1, Accuracy Class row = 1.\n\nPage 4: DN150 (×2), DN200 (×2), DN250, DN300 — class 1.\n\nPage 5: DN25 (×2), DN40 (×2), DN50 (×2), DN65 (×2) — class 2.\n\nPage 6: DN80 (×2), DN100 (×2), DN125 (×2), DN150 (×2) — class 2.\n\nPage 7: DN200, DN250, DN300 — class 2.\n\nSo each DN size has two variants (different Q3/Q1 ratios → different Q1/Q2 values). E.g., DN65 class 1: Q3=100 with ratio 250 (Q1=0.40) OR ratio 160 (Q1=0.625). Wait let me re-read.\n\nPage 3 table:\n- Header: Meter size | DN65 | DN80 | DN80 | DN100 | DN100 | DN125 | DN125\n- Q1: 0,40 | 0,625 | 0,64 | 1,0 | 1,0 | 1,56 | 1,60\n- Q2: 0,64 | 1,00 | 1,02 | 1,6 | 1,6 | 2,50 | 2,56\n- Q3: 100 | 100 | 160 | 160 | 250 | 250 | 400\n- Q4: 125 | 125 | 200 | 200 | 312,5 | 312,5 | 500\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 250 | 160 | 250 | 160 | 250 | 160 | 250\n- Nominal diameter: 65 | 80 | 80 | 100 | 100 | 125 | 125\n\nSo DN65: Q1=0.40, Q3=100, ratio 250.\nDN80: variant A: Q1=0.625, Q3=100, ratio 160; variant B: Q1=0.64, Q3=160, ratio 250.\nDN100: variant A: Q1=1.0, Q3=160, ratio 160; variant B: Q1=1.0, Q3=250, ratio 250.\nDN125: variant A: Q1=1.56, Q3=250, ratio 160; variant B: Q1=1.60, Q3=400, ratio 250.\n\nSo the two variants per DN differ by Q3/Q1 ratio: 160 or 250.\n\nPage 4 (class 1 continued):\n- DN150 (×2), DN200 (×2), DN250, DN300\n- Q1: 2,50 | 2,52 | 3,94 | 4,00 | 6,25 | 10\n- Q2: 4,00 | 4,03 | 6,30 | 6,40 | 10 | 16\n- Q3: 400 | 630 | 630 | 800 | 1000 | 1600\n- Q4: 500 | 787,5 | 787,5 | 1000 | 1250 | 2000\n- Ratio: 160 | 250 | 160 | 200 | 160 | 160\n- Max admissible pressure: 16 | 16 | 16 | 16 | 10 | 10 (16 for DN150–DN200, 10 for DN250/DN300)\n- Verification scale interval: 0,001 for first 5 columns, 0,01 for DN300\n\nSo DN150: A: Q1=2.50, Q3=400, ratio 160; B: Q1=2.52, Q3=630, ratio 250.\nDN200: A: Q1=3.94, Q3=630, ratio 160; B: Q1=4.00, Q3=800, ratio 200.\nDN250: Q1=6.25, Q3=1000, ratio 160.\nDN300: Q1=10, Q3=1600, ratio 160.\n\nPage 5 (class 2): DN25 (×2), DN40 (×2), DN50 (×2), DN65 (×2)\n- Q1: 0,025 | 0,040 | 0,0625 | 0,100 | 0,100 | 0,1575 | 0,1575 | 0,250\n- Q2: 0,040 | 0,064 | 0,100 | 0,160 | 0,160 | 0,252 | 0,250 | 0,400\n- Q3: 10 | 16 | 25 | 40 | 40 | 63 | 63 | 100\n- Q4: 12,5 | 20 | 31,3 | 50 | 50 | 78,75 | 78,75 | 125\n- Ratio: 400 for all (colspan 2 each)\n- VSI: 0,00001 for DN25 (colspan 2), 0,0001 for the rest\n\nDN25: A: Q1=0.025, Q3=10; B: Q1=0.040, Q3=16. Both ratio 400.\nDN40: A: Q1=0.0625, Q3=25; B: Q1=0.100, Q3=40. Ratio 400.\nDN50: A: Q1=0.100, Q3=40; B: Q1=0.1575, Q3=63. Ratio 400.\nDN65: A: Q1=0.1575, Q3=63; B: Q1=0.250, Q3=100. Ratio 400.\n\nHmm interesting — for class 2, ratio is 400 for all, so the two variants differ by Q3 (10 vs 16 etc.). So DN25 class 2: variant with Q3=10 (Q1=0.025) and Q3=16 (Q1=0.040).\n\nWait, ratio 400: Q1 = Q3/400: 10/400 = 0.025 ✓; 16/400 = 0.04 ✓; 25/400 = 0.0625 ✓; 40/400 = 0.1 ✓; 63/400 = 0.1575 ✓; 100/400 = 0.25 ✓. Yes.\n\nSo class 2 variants per DN: two Q3 values (e.g., DN25: Q3=10 or Q3=16; DN40: Q3=25 or 40; DN50: Q3=40 or 63; DN65: Q3=63 or 100; DN80: Q3=100 or 160; DN100: Q3=160 or 250; DN125: Q3=250 or 400; DN150: Q3=400 or 630; DN200: Q3=630; DN250: Q3=1000; DN300: Q3=1600).\n\nPage 6 (class 2): DN80 (×2), DN100 (×2), DN125 (×2), DN150 (×2)\n- Q1: 0,25 | 0,40 | 0,40 | 0,625 | 0,625 | 1,00 | 1,00 | 1,575\n- Q2: 0,40 | 0,64 | 0,64 | 1,00 | 1,00 | 1,60 | 1,60 | 2,52\n- Q3: 100 | 160 | 160 | 250 | 250 | 400 | 400 | 630\n- Q4: 125 | 200 | 200 | 312,5 | 312,5 | 500 | 500 | 787,5\n- Ratio: 400\n- VSI: 0,0001 for DN80, 0,001 for DN100–DN150\n\nPage 7 (class 2): DN200, DN250, DN300\n- Q1: 1,575 | 2,50 | 4,00\n- Q2: 2,52 | 4,00 | 6,40\n- Q3: 630 | 1000 | 1600\n- Q4: 787,5 | 1250 | 2000\n- Ratio: 400\n- Max pressure: 16 for DN200, 10 for DN250/DN300\n- VSI: 0,001 for DN200, DN250; 0,01 for DN300\n\nSo total models: Class 1: DN65 (1), DN80 (2), DN100 (2), DN125 (2), DN150 (2), DN200 (2), DN250 (1), DN300 (1) = 13 variants. Class 2: DN25 (2), DN40 (2), DN50 (2), DN65 (2), DN80 (2), DN100 (2), DN125 (2), DN150 (2), DN200 (1), DN250 (1), DN300 (1) = 18 variants. Total 31 variants.\n\nHow to model model_id? Something like \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100 (Q3/Q1=250)\". Maybe: \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\" or more readable. The variant is distinguished by size + Q3 + ratio. I'll use model_id like \"DN65 class 1, Q3=100 m³/h\" etc. Or simply \"DN65-1-100/250\". Let me settle on a convention: `model_id`: \"DN80 class 1 (Q3=160, Q3/Q1=250)\". Hmm, that's verbose but informative.\n\nActually simpler: since the certificate distinguishes variants only by DN size column groups, I can name models as e.g. \"DN80 class 1 Q3=100\". Let me use: `DN65/C1/Q3=100`, `DN80/C1/Q3=100`, ... Perhaps better readable: \"DN80, class 1, Q3=100 m³/h\".\n\nLet me enumerate all 31:\n\nClass 1 (pages 3-4):\n1. DN65 C1: Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 250, maxP=16, VSI=0.0001\n2. DN80 C1 (a): Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 160, VSI=0.0001\n3. DN80 C1 (b): Q1=0.64, Q2=1.02, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 250, VSI=0.0001\n4. DN100 C1 (a): Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 160, VSI=0.0001\n5. DN100 C1 (b): Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 250, VSI=0.0001\n\nWait — VSI row on page 3: colspan 3 = 0,0001 (DN65, DN80a, DN80b? Actually colspan 3 covers columns 2-4: DN80a, DN80b, DN100a). Let me recompute. The table columns: col1 = row label, then cols 2-8 = DN65, DN80, DN80, DN100, DN100, DN125, DN125 (7 data columns). VSI row: `<td colspan=\"3\">0,0001</td><td colspan=\"4\">0,001</td>`. First colspan 3 covers DN65, DN80a, DN80b; second colspan 4 covers DN100a, DN100b, DN125a, DN125b. So VSI: DN65=0.0001, DN80 both=0.0001, DN100 both=0.001, DN125 both=0.001.\n\nHmm wait, but is that plausible? DN65 Q3=100 VSI 0.0001 m³; DN100 Q3=250 VSI 0.001. Plausible.\n\nPage 3 common: Max pressure 16 for all; temp 0.1/50; indicating range 99.999.999; orientation all positions; env class C; battery 3.6 V; software versions 4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_; accuracy class 1.\n\n6. DN125 C1 (a): Q1=1.56, Q2=2.50, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 160, VSI=0.001\n7. DN125 C1 (b): Q1=1.60, Q2=2.56, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 250, VSI=0.001\n\nPage 4:\n8. DN150 C1 (a): Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 160, maxP=16, VSI=0.001\n9. DN150 C1 (b): Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 250, maxP=16, VSI=0.001\n10. DN200 C1 (a): Q1=3.94, Q2=6.30, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 160, maxP=16, VSI=0.001\n11. DN200 C1 (b): Q1=4.00, Q2=6.40, Q3=800, Q4=1000, ratio 200, maxP=16, VSI=0.001\n12. DN250 C1: Q1=6.25, Q2=10, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 160, maxP=10, VSI=0.001\n13. DN300 C1: Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 160, maxP=10, VSI=0.01\n\nVSI page 4: colspan 5 = 0.001 (DN150a, DN150b, DN200a, DN200b, DN250), DN300 = 0.01.\n\nClass 2 (pages 5-7):\n14. DN25 C2 (a): Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio 400, VSI=0.00001\n15. DN25 C2 (b): Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio 400, VSI=0.00001\n16. DN40 C2 (a): Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.100, Q3=25, Q4=31.3, ratio 400, VSI=0.0001\n17. DN40 C2 (b): Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400, VSI=0.0001\n18. DN50 C2 (a): Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400, VSI=0.0001\n19. DN50 C2 (b): Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400, VSI=0.0001\n20. DN65 C2 (a): Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.250, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400, VSI=0.0001\n21. DN65 C2 (b): Q1=0.250, Q2=0.400, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 400, VSI=0.0001\n22. DN80 C2 (a): Q1=0.25, Q2=0.40, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 400, VSI=0.0001\n23. DN80 C2 (b): Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 400, VSI=0.0001\n24. DN100 C2 (a): Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 400, VSI=0.001\n25. DN100 C2 (b): Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 400, VSI=0.001\n26. DN125 C2 (a): Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 400, VSI=0.001\n27. DN125 C2 (b): Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 400, VSI=0.001\n28. DN150 C2 (a): Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 400, VSI=0.001\n29. DN150 C2 (b): Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 400, VSI=0.001\n30. DN200 C2: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 400, maxP=16, VSI=0.001\n31. DN250 C2: Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 400, maxP=10, VSI=0.001\n32. DN300 C2: Q1=4.00, Q2=6.40, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 400, maxP=10, VSI=0.01\n\nWait VSI page 7: colspan 2 = 0.001 (DN200, DN250), DN300 = 0.01. Yes.\n\nTotal: 13 + 19 = 32 variants. Let me recount class 2: DN25(2), DN40(2), DN50(2), DN65(2), DN80(2), DN100(2), DN125(2), DN150(2), DN200(1), DN250(1), DN300(1) = 2*8 + 3 = 19. Class 1: DN65(1), DN80(2), DN100(2), DN125(2), DN150(2), DN200(2), DN250(1), DN300(1) = 1+2*5+2 = 13. Total 32.\n\nNote: DN50 C2 (a) has Q1=0.100, Q3=40 — same as DN40 C2 (b) (Q1=0.100, Q3=40). Different DN (50 vs 40), so different models.\n\nModel naming: I'll use e.g. \"DN65 class 1 (Q3=100)\" and for DN80: \"DN80 class 1 (Q3=100)\" and \"DN80 class 1 (Q3=160)\". That uniquely identifies each variant since Q3 pairs differ per DN. For class 2, DN25: Q3=10 vs 16; all unique per DN. Yes, Q3 is unique per variant within each class+DN. And class distinguishes (e.g., DN65 C1 Q3=100 ratio 250 vs DN65 C2 Q3=100 ratio 400 — both exist! DN65 C2(b) has Q3=100). So include class: \"DN65, class 1, Q3=100\" vs \"DN65, class 2, Q3=100\". Good, unique.\n\nModel_id format: I'll use \"DN65-C1-Q3=100\" style? Or with spaces. Let me use \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100 m³/h\"? Keep it simple: \"DN65_class1_Q3=100\". Hmm. The R60 example used \"Emax=25t\". I'll use e.g. \"DN80-class1-Q3=160\". I think readable: \"DN65 class 1 (Q3=100)\". I'll go with that.\n\nFor each model, attributes: nominal_diameter (mm), q1, q2, q3, q4 (m³/h), ratio_q3_q1, maximum_admissible_pressure (bar), temperature range, verification_scale_interval (m³), accuracy class... \n\nSince accuracy class varies across models, I could include it in model attributes. The schema has characteristics.model_level[] with attribute × values per model. Alternatively, put per-model attributes in the model entry itself? The schema shows models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. So I can put attributes directly in the model entry! That's cleaner for a table like this. And also emit model_level characteristics for key attributes.\n\nHmm, the schema:\n```\nmodel_family:\n  family_name\n  models[]:\n    model_id, label, attributes{}\ncharacteristics:\n  type_level: {...}\n  model_level[]: {attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}}\n```\n\nThe R137 example shows both matrix_tables AND model_level. For this cert, I think the best approach:\n\n- matrix_tables[]: preserve the 5 tables (one per page) or merge into one? They're really one logical matrix split across pages, split by class. Actually pages 3-4 = class 1; pages 5-7 = class 2. I could emit two matrix_tables: \"Metrological characteristics — accuracy class 1\" (pages 3-4) and \"Metrological characteristics — accuracy class 2\" (pages 5-7). Or preserve 5 separate tables. I think merging into two tables (one per class) is cleaner and lossless.\n\n- model_family.models[]: 32 entries with attributes.\n\n- characteristics.model_level[]: entries for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio, nominal_diameter, max pressure, VSI, accuracy class. That's a lot of repetition but the schema explicitly wants it. With 32 models × 8 attributes, that's heavy but correct. The example says \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". So do all three.\n\nHmm, that will be a very large JSON. Let me think about whether to reduce. The instructions emphasize extraction fidelity. I'll do it fully. To keep the response manageable, model_level entries with compact values arrays.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe put full attributes in models[].attributes{} and keep model_level for the main flow attributes (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio, verification_scale_interval, maximum_admissible_pressure). Type-level common: temperature range 0.1/50 °C, indicating range 99,999,999 m³, orientation all positions, environmental class C, power supply battery 3.6 V, software versions.\n\nCommon attributes across all models → type_level:\n- min_max_admissible_temperature: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, \"°C\"\n- indicating_range: 99999999, \"m³\" (the value \"99.999.999\" uses dots as thousands separators → 99,999,999)\n- orientation: \"All positions\"\n- environmental_class: \"C\"\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery\"\n- battery_voltage (U_battery): 3.6, \"V\"\n- software_versions: [\"4.0.4_\", \"4.0.10_\", \"4.0.11_\"]\n\nNote \"0,1/50\" → min 0.1 max 50 °C.\n\nModel-varying:\n- accuracy_class: 1 or 2 (model_level)\n- nominal_diameter mm\n- q1, q2, q3, q4 m³/h\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 16 or 10 bar\n- verification_scale_interval m³\n\nLet me double check the \"99.999.999\" — indicating range in m³. European format with dots as thousand separators: 99 999 999. Yes.\n\nVerification scale interval values: 0.00001, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.01 m³.\n\nNow Q4 values: 12.5, 20, 31.3, 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 1000, 1250, 2000. Note \"31,3\" → 31.3 (the table shows 31,3 though mathematically 25×1.25=31.25; keep as written: 31.3). And \"78,75\" → 78.75; \"312,5\" → 312.5; \"787,5\" → 787.5.\n\nLet me now carefully build the 32 model records.\n\nClass 1 (13):\n\nM1: DN65 class 1 Q3=100\n- DN: 65, Q1 0.40, Q2 0.64, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 250, maxP 16, VSI 0.0001\n\nM2: DN80 class 1 Q3=100\n- DN 80,