ing/indicating device\" — that's a component description. Should I add components? It says \"equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device\". No type designations for it. I could add a component {role: \"calculating/indicating device\", type_designations: []}. Rule 8: don't fabricate. It's named but no designations. I'll include it minimal: role: \"calculating/indicating device (electronic)\", type_designations: []. Hmm, maybe better to skip since nothing concrete. I'll include one entry since it's genuinely named — actually the schema says \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\". It does name it. I'll include: {\"role\": \"calculating/indicating_device\", \"type_designations\": []}. Also the meter measures forward and reverse flow, has 0D straight inlet/outlet, no flow conditioner — these go into description.\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- module_designation: null (water meters — module concept per R49... not mentioned; omit or null)\n- description: combine page 1 and page 3 text.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- minimum_admissible_temperature / maximum? The row is \"Min/max admissible temperature (°C) 0,1/50\" → one attribute min_max_admissible_temperature: {value: {min:0.1, max:50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}.\n- indicating_range: {value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions\"}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"C\"}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"Battery\"}\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- software_versions: {value: [\"4.0.4_\", \"4.0.10_\", \"4.0.11_\"]} — hmm, value should be number|string. A list of software versions... The rule says lists of values of Emax → model matrix. Software versions aren't models; they apply to all. I'll put value as string \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_\"? Or array. The structured value shape says value: <number | {min,max} | string>. So string: \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_\". Hmm, or maybe list them. I'll use an array? Deviating from shape. I'll use string with semicolons: \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_\". Actually an array seems more semantically right but shape says number|range|string. Keep string.\n\n- measuring_principle: \"electromagnetic\" (from description). Could add.\n- flow_directions: \"forward and reverse\" — from description. Maybe include in description only.\n- straight_inlet_outlet: \"minimum 0 D\" — description.\n- flow_conditioner: \"none\" — description.\n\nI'll keep type_level focused on the table's common rows plus a couple from description. Careful not to over-add. I'll include measuring_principle: \"electromagnetic\" and flow_direction: \"forward and reverse\" maybe. These are legitimately type-level characteristics. I'll add them.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- accuracy_class (unit null): values per model 1 or 2\n- nominal_diameter (mm)\n- minimum_flow_rate_q1 (m³/h)\n- transitional_flow_rate_q2 (m³/h)\n- permanent_flow_rate_q3 (m³/h)\n- overload_flow_rate_q4 (m³/h)\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n- maximum_admissible_pressure (bar)\n- verification_scale_interval (m³)\n\nThat's 9 attributes × 32 values each. Big but fine.\n\nmatrix_tables: I'll emit two merged tables (class 1, class 2) with columns: model, nominal_diameter, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, maximum_admissible_pressure, verification_scale_interval. Plus column_units. And footnotes: none.\n\nAlternatively preserve 5 tables as-is. Merged is cleaner and stated \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — they do fit in model_level, but the example R137 still emitted matrix_tables. I'll emit two matrix_tables entries.\n\nWait, do I need to double-check page 3 Q1 row: \"0,40 | 0,625 | 0,64 | 1,0 | 1,0 | 1,56 | 1,60\" — DN65: 0.40; DN80: 0.625 and 0.64; DN100: 1.0 and 1.0; DN125: 1.56 and 1.60. And ratio row: \"250 | 160 | 250 | 160 | 250 | 160 | 250\": DN65: 250; DN80: 160, 250; DN100: 160, 250; DN125: 160, 250.\n\nCheck Q1 consistency: Q3/ratio: 100/250=0.4 ✓; 100/160=0.625 ✓; 160/25