ue\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n \"indicating_range\": {\"value\": 99999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n \"orientation\": {\"value\": \"All positions\"},\n \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"C\"},\n \"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"Battery\"},\n \"battery_voltage\": {\"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n \"software_versions\": {\"value\": \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_\"}\n}\n\nHmm \"straight_inlet_outlet\" value \"minimum 0 D\" — string ok. flow_conditioner \"none\" ok.\n\nUnit ids: leave null. I'll include \"unit_id\": null in structured values? Shape says include; example in R137 model_level omitted unit_id. Rule 3: \"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\". I'll include unit_id: null in type_level values for completeness. For model_level attribute entries, example shows just unit_symbol; I'll match example (attribute, unit_symbol, values). Values inside model_level are plain numbers in example ({\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}). I'll follow the example format: values as {\"model\": ..., \"value\": N} without nested unit objects. Good — that matches the R137 example and keeps size down.\n\nmatrix_tables description: mention source pages.\n\nTable 1: name \"Metrological characteristics — accuracy class 1 (pages 3–4)\". columns as above. rows 13.\nTable 2: name \"Metrological characteristics — accuracy class 2 (pages 5–7)\". rows 19.\n\nfootnotes: [] omit.\n\nAlso add \"column_units\".\n\nLet me write the final JSON now. I must be careful it's valid JSON (no trailing commas, proper quoting, ³ characters fine).\n\nAlso \"date_issued\": \"2011-05-02\".\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" both at certificate and issuing_authority.\n\nDouble-check applicant name: \"KROHNE Altometer\". Yes.\n\nEmail certin@nmi.nl; phone +31 78 6332332; website www.nmi.nl; person_responsible \"C. Oosterman\"; person_title \"Head Certification Board\".\n\nNow, writing it all out. Let me create the model_level arrays.\n\nModel IDs in order (I'll fix one orde